Q: SwiftData - limit to one of each matching query A: <p>Try this approach using a computed property <code>uniqueNames</code>
to get the items, one for each of the 25 <code>itemName</code>.
Then a <code>Picker</code> as user-facing filter to choose sorting by <code>itemName</code> and/or <code>timestamp</code>.</p>
<p>Adjust this logic for your purpose.</p>
<p>Example code where the <code>Query</code> already sort the <code>items</code> by <code>timestamp</code>:</p>
<pre><code>struct DetailView: View {
    @Query(sort: \LogItem.timestamp, order: .reverse) private var items: [LogItem]

    // items are already sorted by timestamp
    var uniqueNames: [LogItem] {
        items.reduce(into: []) { result, log in
            if !result.contains(where: { $0.itemName == log.itemName }) {
                result.append(log)
            }
        }
    }

    @State private var sorter: String = &quot;timestamp&quot;

    var body: some View {
        VStack {
            Picker(&quot;Sort by&quot;, selection: $sorter) {
                Text(&quot;Timestamp&quot;).tag(&quot;timestamp&quot;)
                Text(&quot;Item Name&quot;).tag(&quot;itemName&quot;)
            }
            .pickerStyle(SegmentedPickerStyle())

            List(uniqueNames.sorted { lhs, rhs in
                sorter == &quot;timestamp&quot;
                ? lhs.timestamp &gt; rhs.timestamp
                : lhs.itemName &lt; rhs.itemName}) { item in
                    HStack {
                        Text(item.itemName)
                        Text(item.timestamp, format: .dateTime)
                    }
                }

        }
    }
}
</code></pre>

Q: server for a Chess project in c++ A: <p>I used <a href="https://github.com/drogonframework/drogon" rel="nofollow noreferrer">drogon</a> framework to build non trivial server backend in C++. It is easy to use:</p>
<pre><code>void TestCtrl::asyncHandleHttpRequest(
  const HttpRequestPtr&amp; req,
  std::function&lt;void (const HttpResponsePtr &amp;)&gt; &amp;&amp;callback)
{
    auto resp = HttpResponse::newHttpResponse();
    resp-&gt;setBody(&quot;&lt;p&gt;Hello, world!&lt;/p&gt;&quot;);
    callback(resp);
}
</code></pre>
<p>Full minimal setup can be found under the link but complete simpler server easily fits in one file. If the core functionality server delivers is written in C++, having C++ server as well may not sound completely irrational.</p>
<p>C++ on the browser side is not entirely impossble and likely can be implemented on <a href="https://developer.fermyon.com/wasm-languages/cpp" rel="nofollow noreferrer">WebAssembly</a>. I do not know details however because I myself used Rust (<a href="https://crates.io/crates/yew" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Yew</a> for front end).</p>

